### Task 12 requests: N:M (product <-> supplier), 1:1 (customer <-> profile) and
### inheritance (CompanyCustomer extends Customer), for the VS Code "REST Client"
### extension (humao.rest-client) or Postman. Run them top to bottom: later requests
### use the ids created by earlier ones.
### To see the SQL Hibernate runs, start the API with:
###   ./mvnw spring-boot:run -Dspring-boot.run.arguments=--logging.level.org.hibernate.SQL=DEBUG

@base = http://localhost:8080/api


######################################################################
### N:M  Product <-> Supplier  (join table product_suppliers)
######################################################################

### Example: all products of supplier 1 (primarySupplier = false means linked only via the join table)
GET {{base}}/suppliers/1/products

### All suppliers of product 1 (the demo data gave products 1-20 two suppliers)
GET {{base}}/products/1/suppliers

### Save: link supplier 3 to product 1 -> insert into product_suppliers
POST {{base}}/products/1/suppliers/3

### Product 1 now has three suppliers
GET {{base}}/products/1/suppliers

### ...and supplier 3's products now include product 1
GET {{base}}/suppliers/3/products

### Linking the same pair again: 409
POST {{base}}/products/1/suppliers/3

### Delete: unlink supplier 3 -> deletes only that one row from product_suppliers
DELETE {{base}}/products/1/suppliers/3

### Back to two suppliers; supplier 3 itself still exists
GET {{base}}/products/1/suppliers

### Unlinking a pair that isn't linked: 404
DELETE {{base}}/products/1/suppliers/3

### Deleting a product removes its join rows. Create a product (primary supplier 59)...
# @name nmProduct
POST {{base}}/products
Content-Type: application/json

{ "name": "Testituote N:M", "description": "Kahden toimittajan tuote", "price": 9.90, "stockQuantity": 5, "categoryId": 1, "supplierId": 59 }

### ...link suppliers 59 and 60 to it
POST {{base}}/products/{{nmProduct.response.body.$.id}}/suppliers/59

###
POST {{base}}/products/{{nmProduct.response.body.$.id}}/suppliers/60

### Supplier 60's products include the new product
GET {{base}}/suppliers/60/products

### Delete the product: Hibernate deletes its product_suppliers rows first, then the product
DELETE {{base}}/products/{{nmProduct.response.body.$.id}}

### Supplier 60 still exists, the deleted product is gone from its list
GET {{base}}/suppliers/60/products


######################################################################
### 1:1  Customer <-> CustomerProfile  (shared primary key)
######################################################################

### Fetch an existing profile (customers 1-50 have one from the demo data)
GET {{base}}/customers/10/profile

### Customer 51 has no profile: 404
GET {{base}}/customers/51/profile

### Create a new customer for the save/delete tests
# @name profileCustomer
POST {{base}}/customers
Content-Type: application/json

{ "firstName": "Pekka", "lastName": "Profiili", "email": "pekka@example.fi", "phone": "040-3333333" }

### No profile yet: 404
GET {{base}}/customers/{{profileCustomer.response.body.$.id}}/profile

### Save: create the profile (saved through the cascade, customerId = the customer's id)
PUT {{base}}/customers/{{profileCustomer.response.body.$.id}}/profile
Content-Type: application/json

{ "birthDate": "1990-05-17", "preferredLanguage": "fi", "marketingConsent": true, "loyaltyPoints": 120 }

### Fetch it
GET {{base}}/customers/{{profileCustomer.response.body.$.id}}/profile

### Update: the same row is updated, not replaced
PUT {{base}}/customers/{{profileCustomer.response.body.$.id}}/profile
Content-Type: application/json

{ "birthDate": "1990-05-17", "preferredLanguage": "en", "marketingConsent": false, "loyaltyPoints": 250 }

### Delete only the profile (orphanRemoval): the customer stays
DELETE {{base}}/customers/{{profileCustomer.response.body.$.id}}/profile

### Profile gone: 404 ...
GET {{base}}/customers/{{profileCustomer.response.body.$.id}}/profile

### ... customer still there
GET {{base}}/customers/{{profileCustomer.response.body.$.id}}

### Create the profile again, then delete the whole customer: the profile goes too (cascade)
PUT {{base}}/customers/{{profileCustomer.response.body.$.id}}/profile
Content-Type: application/json

{ "birthDate": "1990-05-17", "marketingConsent": true, "loyaltyPoints": 10 }

###
DELETE {{base}}/customers/{{profileCustomer.response.body.$.id}}

### Check in MariaDB that no profile row is left:
###   SELECT * FROM customer_profiles WHERE customer_id = <id>;


######################################################################
### Inheritance  CompanyCustomer extends Customer  (JOINED)
######################################################################

### Polymorphic fetch: customer 55 is a company customer -> type "CompanyCustomer" + company details
GET {{base}}/customers/55

### Customer 1 is a plain customer -> type "Customer", company null
GET {{base}}/customers/1

### Company customers only (51-60 from the demo data)
GET {{base}}/company-customers

### Save: one request inserts a customers row and a company_customers row with the same id
# @name company
POST {{base}}/company-customers
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "firstName": "Liisa",
  "lastName": "Laskuttaja",
  "email": "liisa@yritys.fi",
  "phone": "09-1234567",
  "addresses": [
    { "streetAddress": "Teollisuuskatu 10", "postalCode": "00510", "city": "Helsinki", "country": "Suomi" }
  ],
  "companyName": "Testiyritys Oy",
  "businessId": "2345678-9",
  "vatNumber": "FI23456789",
  "invoiceReference": "KP-500"
}

### Fetch it through the common customer endpoint
GET {{base}}/customers/{{company.response.body.$.id}}

### The same business ID again: 409
POST {{base}}/company-customers
Content-Type: application/json

{ "firstName": "Toinen", "lastName": "Henkilö", "email": "toinen@yritys.fi", "companyName": "Kopio Oy", "businessId": "2345678-9" }

### Badly formatted business ID: 400
POST {{base}}/company-customers
Content-Type: application/json

{ "firstName": "Väärä", "lastName": "Tunnus", "email": "vaara@yritys.fi", "companyName": "Väärä Oy", "businessId": "123" }

### Delete: removes the company_customers row and the customers row (and the address)
DELETE {{base}}/customers/{{company.response.body.$.id}}

### Gone: 404
GET {{base}}/customers/{{company.response.body.$.id}}
